<% const shellsRepo = 'https://github.com/h2h-project/turtle_Shells'; %>
<% const bambooGallery = [
  { src: '/images/ecojoiners/assembly-777px.webp', alt: 'Assembling a bamboo ecojoiner' },
  { src: '/images/ecojoiners/cube-777px.webp', alt: 'A completed bamboo ecojoiner cube' },
  { src: '/images/ecojoiners/ecojoiners-and-briks-777px.webp', alt: 'Bamboo ecojoiners connecting ecobricks' },
  { src: '/images/ecojoiners/cube-table-777px.webp', alt: 'A table built from cubed ecobricks and ecojoiners' },
  { src: '/images/ecojoiners/coffee-table-777px.webp', alt: 'A coffee table made with bamboo ecojoiners' }
]; %>
<%- include('partials/header', { pageTitle, theme, lang, direction }) %>
<%- include('partials/nav') %>
<main id="main" class="ecojoiners-page">
  <section class="page-banner">
    <img class="page-banner__bg" src="/images/ecojoiner-background.webp" alt="" aria-hidden="true" />
    <div class="page-banner__inner">
      <div class="page-banner__heading">
        <h1 class="page-banner__title">Turtle Ecojoiner</h1>
        <p class="page-banner__lead">
          The regenerative heart of every Hope Turtle — a wood-slat frame that binds
          food-filled bottles into a seaworthy shell.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="page-banner__actions">
        <a class="button" href="<%= shellsRepo %>" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
          <i class="fa-brands fa-github" aria-hidden="true"></i> Get the flatpack files
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="layout">
    <section class="page-split">
      <div class="page-split__main">
        <p>
          The heart of the turtle is something we call an “ecojoiner”.
          <a href="https://ecobricks.org/en/ecojoiners" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Ecojoiners</a>
          were originally developed by the Global Ecobrick Alliance as a means to connect
          ecobricks into super-practical cubic matrixes. Arranged this way, cubes of ecobricks
          can be built into chairs, tables, shelves, and all sorts of furniture — and they can
          also serve as the substructure for
          <a href="https://ecobricks.org/en/earth.php" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">earth and ecobrick walls</a>.
        </p>

        <p>
          The human2human project has put ecojoiners to use for a new purpose: holding
          hope-bottles together. It turns out that just as PET bottles are fantastic for
          containing unrecyclable plastic to make ecobricks, they're also great for containing
          dry food of all kinds in marine conditions!
        </p>

        <p>
          Using ecojoiners, a Hope Turtle can hold five to six 1.5-litre bottles packed with
          food. We've tested it — and because bottles seal so well, even in the ocean, they can
          preserve their contents for weeks! Optionally, the sixth bottle can be what we call a
          <em>control bottle</em>: a cut 1.5L PET bottle that houses our specially designed sail
          mechanism, microcontroller unit, and power system.
        </p>

        <p>
          The Global Ecobrick Alliance first tried making ecojoiners out of recycled plastic
          (failure!), then bamboo (way better!), and finally settled on a design that uses wood
          slats. Unlike bamboo, wood can be sourced just about anywhere in the world. As long as
          it's the right size — slats from shipping pallets, plywood, or thin boards — it can be
          transformed with a band saw into a set of flat slats that assemble into our hexa-cube
          ecojoiner.
        </p>

        <aside class="ecojoiners-note">
          <h2>Special note on 1.5L bottles</h2>
          <p>
            Did you know you can find 85mm 1.5L bottles just about anywhere in the world? Yep —
            our team has researched it! The brand may vary, but without fail, 1.5L water bottles
            tend toward a diameter of 85mm. So far we've been able to find matching bottles in
            pretty much every country we've needed to. This is important: consequently, we've
            designed our ecojoiners to be compatible with 1.5L bottles with short bottle necks.
          </p>
        </aside>

        <h2>Build your own</h2>
        <p>
          Access the latest source files for the ecojoiner flatpack:
        </p>
        <div class="ecojoiners-actions">
          <a class="button" href="<%= shellsRepo %>" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
            <i class="fa-brands fa-github" aria-hidden="true"></i> turtle_Shells source files
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <aside class="page-split__aside">
        <figure class="ecojoiners-figure">
          <img src="/images/wood-ecojoiner.webp" alt="A wood-slat turtle ecojoiner holding food bottles together" loading="lazy" />
        </figure>
        <figure class="ecojoiners-figure">
          <img src="/images/help-build.webp" alt="Building a Hope Turtle with wood-slat ecojoiners" loading="lazy" />
        </figure>
      </aside>
    </section>

    <section class="eco-gallery-section">
      <h2>The original bamboo ecojoiners</h2>
      <div class="eco-gallery">
        <% bambooGallery.forEach(function (image) { %>
          <figure class="eco-gallery__item">
            <img src="<%= image.src %>" alt="<%= image.alt %>" loading="lazy" />
          </figure>
        <% }) %>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</main>
<%- include('partials/footer') %>
